ne because any line through two of them has a forbidden slope.  Contradiction.\n\n* **\\(\\ell:x=3\\).**  \n  Points on \\(\\ell\\): \\((3,1)\\) only.  \n  The four remaining points are handled exactly as in the case \\(\\ell:y=3\\); at most two sunny lines can each cover two points, leaving one uncovered.  \n\nThus no vertical \\(\\ell\\) works.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  \\(\\ell\\) of slope \\(-1\\)\n\n* **\\(\\ell:x+y=2\\).**  \n  Points on \\(\\ell\\): \\((1,1)\\) only.  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  The only admissible pairs are \\((1,2)-(3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)) and \\((1,3)-(2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)).  \n  These two sunny lines cover four of the five points, leaving \\((2,2)\\) uncovered – contradiction.\n\n* **\\(\\ell:x+y=3\\).**  \n  Points on \\(\\ell\\): \\((1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  The only admissible pair among them is \\((1,1)-(2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).  \n  After using this sunny line, the two points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) remain; any line through them has slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden.  Hence a second sunny line cannot cover both, contradiction.\n\n* **\\(\\ell:x+y=4\\).**  \n  Points on \\(\\ell\\): \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).  \n  Remaining points: \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\).  \n  Every pair among these three points has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently no sunny line can contain two of them.  With only two sunny lines we can cover at most two of the three points, leaving one uncovered – contradiction.\n\nThus no line of slope \\(-1\\) can serve as the unique non‑sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nSince every possible choice for the single non‑sunny line leads to a contradiction, a set of three distinct lines covering all points of \\(P\\) cannot contain exactly two sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, a configuration