ses through the point \\((k+3,1)\\) (for \\(m_1\\)), through \\((1,k+3)\\) (for \\(m_2\\)) and through \\((k+2,2)\\) (for \\(m_3\\)).  \nExplicitly these lines are\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{k+1}&:\\; y-1 = 2\\,(x-(k+3)),\\\\\nL_{k+2}&:\\; y-(k+3) = -2\\,(x-1),\\\\\nL_{k+3}&:\\; y-2 = \\tfrac12\\,(x-(k+2)).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA straightforward calculation shows:\n\n* each line in (4) contains exactly \\(k+3\\) points of \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\);\n* none of the three lines meets any point of the inner triangle \\(T_k\\);\n* the three slopes are \\(2,-2,\\tfrac12\\), all different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nConsequently the union of the three new lines covers the whole outer part \\(A\\cup B\\cup C\\).  \nAdding them to the previously existing family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) yields a family \\(\\mathcal L_{k+3}\\) of \\(k+3\\) sunny lines that covers \\(T_{k+3}\\).\n\nThus, by induction, a covering exists for every integer \\(k\\ge0\\) with \\(k\\neq2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Completion of the original problem\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) be given.\n\n*If \\(k=2\\):* by Section 2 no configuration exists.\n\n*If \\(k\\neq2\\) and \\(0\\le k\\le n\\):*  \nPlace the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (1).  \nBy Section 3 there is a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the remaining triangle \\(T_k\\).  \nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct, every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them, and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nHence for each \\(n\\ge3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. all non‑negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\) except \\(k=2\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as 