ion and therefore is not justified.\n\n---\n\n## Detailed Verification Log\n\nBelow each quoted passage from the proposed solution, I assess its correctness and note any errors.\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Definitions and Reduction\n\n> \u201cLet  \n> \\[\n> S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n> \\qquad \n> |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n> \\]  \n> A line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  The set \\(S_n\\) is exactly the collection of lattice points required by the problem, and its cardinality is \\(\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\).  The definition of \u201csunny\u2019\u2019 matches the statement.\n\n---\n\n> \u201cTake any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n> Place the \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines  \n> \\[\n> x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n> \\tag{1}\n> \\]  \n> \u2026 The points that are not yet covered are precisely  \n> \\[\n> T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n> \\tag{2}\n> \\]  \n> the right\u2011angled triangle of size \\(k\\).\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  The vertical lines cover all points with first coordinate \\(a\\le n-k\\).  Substituting \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) shows that the uncovered points satisfy \\(a'+b\\le k+1\\), i.e. they form \\(T_k\\).  Hence the reduction to covering \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is valid.\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\n> \u201c\\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n> The three possible lines through two of these points are  \n> \\[\n> x=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=3,\n> \\]  \n> whose slopes are \\(\\infty,0,-1\\); all are non\u2011sunny.  \n> Hence a sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(T_2\\).  \n> With only two sunny lines we could cover at most two points, leaving one uncovered.  \n> Thus **no** configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\u201d\n\n*Correct.*  The enumeration of the three lines is exhaustive, and each is non\u2011sunny.  Consequently any sunny line can contain at