eft uncovered. The third point would require a third sunny line, contradicting the requirement \u201cexactly\u202f\\(k=2\\) sunny lines\u201d.  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the reduction (1) leaves the whole set \\(S_3\\) equal to \\(T_2\\); an explicit configuration with two sunny lines and one non\u2011sunny line is\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=2x,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;x=2 .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(k=2\\) is possible only when \\(n=3\\).\n\n3. **Explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,3\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\); all of them are non\u2011sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non\u2011sunny) and one sunny line \\(y=x\\).  \n   The vertical lines cover every point with first coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\); the remaining points are \\((n,1)\\) and \\((n-1,2)\\), both of which lie on the line \\(y=x\\).\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) and the three sunny lines\n   \\[\n   L_1:\\;y=x,\\qquad\n   L_2:\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n   L_3:\\;y=-2x+5 .\n   \\]\n   One checks directly that the three lines together cover the six points of the triangle \\(T_3\\); the vertical lines cover the rest of \\(S_n\\).\n\n4. **A family of constructions for many larger values of\u202f\\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\ge 3\\) be a multiple of\u202f3, say \\(k=3t\\).  \n   After the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (1) the uncovered set is \\(T_k\\).  \n   Partition the anti\u2011diagonal of \\(T_k\\) (the set \\(\\{(i,k+2-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k+1\\}\\)) into \\(t\\) blocks of three consecutive points:\n   \\[\n   B_j=\\bigl\\{(3j-2,\\,k+4-3j),\\;(3j-1,\\,k+3-3j),\\;(3j,\\,k+2-3j)\\bigr\\},\n   \\qquad j=1,\\dots ,t .\n   \\]\n   For each block \\(B_j\\) we introduce three sunny lines with slopes \\(2,-2,\\tfrac12\\) that pass respectively through the three points of the block; the explicit equations are\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_{j,1}&:\