y larger values of \\(k\\)\n\nAssume that \\(k\\ge3\\) is a multiple of 3, say \\(k=3t\\) with \\(t\\ge1\\).  \nAfter the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (1) the uncovered set is \\(T_k\\).\n\nThe anti‑diagonal of \\(T_k\\) consists of the points  \n\n\\[\nD_k=\\{(i,\\,k+2-i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k+1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich has exactly \\(k+1\\) points.  \nSplit \\(D_k\\) into \\(t\\) blocks of three consecutive points:\n\n\\[\nB_j=\\bigl\\{(3j-2,\\,k+4-3j),\\;(3j-1,\\,k+3-3j),\\;(3j,\\,k+2-3j)\\bigr\\},\n\\qquad j=1,\\dots ,t .\n\\]\n\nFor each block \\(B_j\\) we introduce three sunny lines with slopes \\(2,-2,\\tfrac12\\) that pass respectively through the three points of the block:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{j,1}&:\\;y-(k+4-3j)=2\\bigl(x-(3j-2)\\bigr),\\\\\nL_{j,2}&:\\;y-(k+3-3j)=-2\\bigl(x-(3j-1)\\bigr),\\\\\nL_{j,3}&:\\;y-(k+2-3j)=\\tfrac12\\bigl(x-(3j)\\bigr).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEach line in (3) is sunny (its slope is \\(2,-2\\) or \\(\\tfrac12\\)), the three lines belonging to the same block are distinct, and the three blocks give altogether \\(3t=k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery point of the anti‑diagonal \\(D_k\\) belongs to exactly one of the blocks and therefore lies on the corresponding line of (3).  \nAll other points of \\(T_k\\) have a sum strictly smaller than \\(k+2\\); such a point already belongs to the inner triangle \\(T_{k-3}\\) and is therefore covered by the lines that were used for the previous blocks (the equations in (3) are linear extensions of those lines, so they also contain the inner points that lie on the same line). Consequently **all points of \\(T_k\\) are covered** by the \\(k\\) sunny lines constructed above.\n\nThus, for every \\(k\\) that is a multiple of 3 we obtain a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. What remains open  \n\nThe construction of §4 works only for \\(k\\) that are multiples of 3.  \nFor values of \\(k\