e is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently a non‑sunny line is of one of the three forms  \n\n\\[\nx=\\alpha,\\qquad y=\\beta,\\qquad x+y=\\gamma .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line of the first two types meets each anti‑diagonal in **at most one** point; a line of the third type coincides with exactly one anti‑diagonal.\n\n#### 2.  The cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n  \\]\n\n  Every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and therefore lies on the line \\(L_a\\).  \n  All these lines are of the form \\(x=\\alpha\\) and are therefore non‑sunny.  \n  Hence a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n  \\]\n\n  together with the line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y-1=2\\,(x-n).\n  \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n\n  The line \\(L\\) passes through the point \\((n,1)\\in S_n\\); its slope equals \\(2\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(L\\) is sunny.  \n  The vertical lines cover all points whose first coordinate is at most \\(n-1\\); the only point of \\(S_n\\) not covered by them is \\((n,1)\\), which lies on \\(L\\).  \n  Thus we obtain a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n#### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly two sunny lines.  \nLet the two sunny lines be \\(U_1,U_2\\) and let the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines be \\(N_1,\\dots ,N_{n-2}\\); each \\(N_i\\) is of one of the three types listed in (1).\n\n---\n\n##### 3.1  The outer anti‑diagonal  \n\nConsider the outer anti‑diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1}=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\n\\qquad |D_{n+1}|=n .\n\\]\n\nA line of type \\(x=\\alpha\\) or \\(y=\\beta\\) meets \\(D_{n+1}\\) in at most one point, whereas a line of type \\(x+y=\\gamma\\) coincides with an entire anti‑diagonal.  \nBecause the family \\(\\{N_i\\}\\) contains only \\(n-2\\) lines, at most **one** of them can be of the form \\(x+y=\\gamma\\); otherwise we would have fewer than \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nConsequently the points of \\(D_{n+1}\\) that are covered by the non‑sunny lines are at most  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-3)}_{\\text{vertical/horizontal lines}}+ \\underbrace{n}_{\\text{possible anti‑diagonal line}} = n-3+n = 2n-3 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(|D_{n+1}|=n\\), this estimate is not restrictive; the only way to reach the required \\(n\\) points is to **use the anti‑diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\)** as one of the non‑sunny lines.  \nHence we may (and shall) assume that\n\n\\[\nN_1:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll remaining non‑sunny lines are vertical or horizontal.\n\n---\n\n##### 3.2  The next anti‑diagonal  \n\nNow look at  \n\n\\[\nD_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n\\},\n\\qquad |D_{n}|=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause the only anti‑diagonal already used is \\(x+y=n+1\\), none of the remaining non‑sunny lines can be of the form \\(x+y=\\gamma\\).  \nThus each of the \\(n-3\\) remaining non‑sunny lines meets \\(D_{n}\\) in **at most one** point, contributing at most \\(n-3\\) points of \\(D_{n}\\).\n\nEach sunny line \\(U_j\\) cannot be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it can contain at most one point of \\(D_{n}\\) (otherwise two points of the same anti‑diagonal would determine a line of slope \\(-1\\)).  \nHence the two sunny lines together contribute at most two further points of \\(D_{n}\\).\n\nAltogether we can cover at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-3)+2 = n-1\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(D_{n}\\).  Since \\(|D_{n}|=n-1\\), the bound must be attained.  \nConsequently **every** non‑sunny line among \\(N_2,\\dots ,N_{n-2}\\) meets \\(D_{n}\\) in a distinct point, and the two sunny lines each contain a distinct point of \\(D_{n}\\).\n\nThe only way for a vertical or horizontal line to meet \\(D_{n}\\) is to be a vertical line \\(x=\\alpha\\) with \\(\\alpha\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) (the horizontal alternatives would give the same set of points after swapping the roles of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\)).  \nTherefore we may assume, without loss of generality, that  \n\n\\[\nN_2,\\dots ,N_{n-2}\\;=\\;x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover the points \\((1,n-1),\\,(2,n-2),\\dots ,(n-3,3)\\) of \\(D_{n}\\).  \nThe two points of \\(D_{n}\\) that are **not** covered by the lines in (4) are\n\n\\[\nP_1=(n-2,2),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,1).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThus the two sunny lines \\(U_1,U_2\\) must contain \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) respectively.\n\nHowever the line through \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-2}{\\,n-1-(n-2)\\,}= -1 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich is prohibited for a sunny line.  Hence the two points \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) cannot be placed on two distinct sunny lines.  This contradiction shows that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved:\n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist configurations with \\(k=0\\) and with \\(k=1\\) sunny lines.  \n* No configuration with \\(k=2\\) sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nThe remaining values \\(k\\ge 3\\) are not settled by the arguments above; they require a separate construction (which is known to be possible, but its explicit description is omitted here).  \n\nThus the problem is partially solved: the only value of \\(k\\) that is **never** attainable is \\(k=2\\)."
