point \\((n,1)\\in S_n\\); its slope equals \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(U\\) is sunny.  \n  Every point of \\(S_n\\) with first coordinate \\(\\le n-1\\) lies on one of the vertical lines, while \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(U\\).  \n  Thus we have a configuration with exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Non\u2011existence of a configuration with \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(S_n\\) and contains exactly two sunny lines.  \nLet the two sunny lines be \\(U_1,U_2\\) and let the remaining \\(n-2\\) lines be \\(N_1,\\dots ,N_{\\,n-2}\\); each \\(N_i\\) is of one of the three forms (1).\n\nConsider the outer anti\u2011diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+1}=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n+1\\},\n\\qquad |D_{n+1}|=n .\n\\]\n\nA line of type \\(x=\\alpha\\) or \\(y=\\beta\\) meets \\(D_{n+1}\\) in at most one point; a line of type \\(x+y=\\gamma\\) coincides with an entire anti\u2011diagonal.  \nBecause we have only \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines, at most **one** of them can be of the form \\(x+y=\\gamma\\); otherwise we would have fewer than \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n\nHence the \\(n-2\\) non\u2011sunny lines together can cover at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \nEach sunny line is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it can contain at most one point of \\(D_{n+1}\\).  \nConsequently at most \\(n+1\\) points of \\(D_{n+1}\\) could be covered.  \nSince \\(|D_{n+1}|=n\\), the only way to achieve a full covering of \\(D_{n+1}\\) is that **one** of the non\u2011sunny lines is exactly the anti\u2011diagonal  \n\n\\[\nN_1:\\;x+y=n+1 .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nNow look at the next anti\u2011diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=n\\},\n\\qquad |D_{n}|=n-1 .\n\\]\n\nNo further line of the form \\(x+y=\\gamma\\) is available, so each of the remaining \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines meets \\(D_{n}\\) in at most one point, contributing at most \\(n-3\\) points.  \nThe two sunny lines can each contribute at most one point of \\(D_{n}\\); therefore at most \\(n-1\\) points of \\(D_{n}\\) can be covered.  \nEquality forces the \\(n-3\\) non\u2011sunny lines to be the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-3 .\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWith (4) the points of \\(D_{n}\\) that remain uncovered are  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(n-2,2),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,1).\n\\]\n\nBoth must lie on the two sunny lines.  However the line through \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) has slope  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{1-2}{(n-1)-(n-2)}=-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is forbidden for a sunny line.  Hence the two sunny lines cannot simultaneously contain \\(P_1\\) and \\(P_2\\) \u2013 a contradiction.\n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for every \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall build \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n#### 3.1  Non\u2011sunny vertical lines\n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n-k). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate belongs to \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Reduction to a smaller triangle\n\nThe points that are still uncovered form  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAfter the translation \\(a'=a-(n-k)\\) the set \\(T_k\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\nT_k'=\\{(a',b)\\mid 1\\le a'\\le k,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\}=S_{k}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus we have reduced the problem to covering the triangle \\(S_{k}\\).\n\n#### 3.3  An auxiliary anti\u2011diagonal\n\nAdd the non\u2011sunny line  \n\n\\[\nA:\\;x+y=n+1 .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nIn the picture (6) this line coincides with the outer anti\u2011diagonal of \\(S_{k}\\), i.e. the set \\(\\{(a',b)\\mid a'+b=k+1\\}\\).  \nRemoving these points leaves the smaller triangle  \n\n\\[\nS_{k-1}= \\{(a',b)\\mid 1\\le a'\\le k-1,\\;a'+b\\le k\\},\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(\\frac{(k-1)k}{2}\\) points.\n\n#### 3.4  All\u2011sunny covering of \\(S_{k-1}\\)\n\nWe prove by induction on \\(m\\ge 2\\) the following statement.\n\n> **Lemma 1.** For every integer \\(m\\ge 2\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain all points of \\(S_{m}\\).\n\n*Proof by induction.*\n\n*Base \\(m=2\\).*  \nThe two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=2x-2\n\\]\n\nhave slopes \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and contain the three points \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) of \\(S_{2}\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the lemma holds for \\(m-1\\) (\\(m\\ge 3\\)); let \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{m-1}\\}\\) be a sunny covering of \\(S_{m-1}\\).  \nConsider the new anti\u2011diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_{m+1}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=m+1\\},\\qquad |D_{m+1}|=m .\n\\]\n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL_m:\\;y=(m-1)x-(m-2). \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nIts slope \\(m-1\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so \\(L_m\\) is sunny.  \nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le m\\),\n\n\\[\n(m+1-a)=(m-1)a-(m-2),\n\\]\n\nhence \\((a,m+1-a)\\in L_m\\); consequently \\(L_m\\) contains the whole anti\u2011diagonal \\(D_{m+1}\\).  \nSince none of the previously constructed lines has slope \\(-1\\), each of them meets \\(D_{m+1}\\) in at most one point.  \nTherefore the family \\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\\) covers all points of \\(S_{m}\\) and consists solely of sunny lines. \u220e\n\nApplying Lemma\u202f1 with \\(m=k-1\\) yields \\(k-1\\) sunny lines that cover the triangle \\(S_{k-1}\\) defined in (8).\n\n#### 3.5  Assembling the whole configuration\n\nCollect the following lines:\n\n* the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (5);\n* the anti\u2011diagonal line (7);\n* the \\(k-1\\) sunny lines given by Lemma\u202f1 for the triangle \\(S_{k-1}\\).\n\nThe total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\n(n-k)+1+(k-1)=n,\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny (the \\(k-1\\) lines from Lemma\u202f1 together with the line \\(L_{k-1}\\) that appears in the inductive construction).  \nAll points of \\(S_n\\) are covered: the vertical lines handle the left part, the anti\u2011diagonal handles the outer anti\u2011diagonal of the right part, and the sunny lines from Lemma\u202f1 handle the remaining interior points.  \nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a required configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\nCombining the results of Sections\u202f1\u20133 we obtain:\n\n* \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are realizable (explicit constructions).  \n* No configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists (Lemma\u202f2).  \n* For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) a configuration exists (Lemma\u202f3).\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"

