ing \\(n-2\\) lines be non\u2011sunny.  \n   Remove all non\u2011sunny vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=t\\) (where \\(t\\) is the number of vertical non\u2011sunny lines).  \n   The points that are still uncovered form a smaller triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{m}=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge t+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad m=n-t .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the problem reduces to covering the triangle \\(S_m\\) with exactly two sunny lines.  \n   For \\(m\\ge 3\\) the triangle \\(S_m\\) contains at least three points on the outer diagonal \\(D_{m+1}\\); a sunny line can meet this diagonal in at most one point, so two sunny lines can cover at most two points of \\(D_{m+1}\\). Consequently the outer diagonal cannot be completely covered, a contradiction.  \n   The only way to avoid the contradiction is \\(m=2\\), i.e. \\(t=n-2\\).  \n   When \\(m=2\\) the remaining points are \\(\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\). Any line that contains two of them is either vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\); therefore it is non\u2011sunny. Hence two sunny lines cannot cover the three points, again a contradiction.  \n\n   Thus a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge4\\). (For \\(n=3\\) the construction \\(\\{x=1,\\;L_1:y=2x-1,\\;L_2:y=2x-2\\}\\) shows that \\(k=2\\) is possible.)\n\n5. **Partial progress toward a construction for \\(k\\ge3\\).**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\ge3\\) and put \\(t=n-k\\).  \n   Use the \\(t\\) vertical non\u2011sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   After deleting these columns the uncovered points form the triangle \\(S_k\\).  \n   The problem is therefore reduced to the following statement, which I have not yet been able to prove in full generality:\n\n   > **Lemma (still conjectural).**  \n   > For every integer \\(m\\ge3\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that together contain all points of the triangle \\(S_m\\).\n\n   I have verified the lemma directly for \\(m=3\\) (the three lines  \n\n  