1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid x=c\\text{ is one of the lines}\\},\n   \\qquad\n   H=\\{c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\mid y=c\\text{ is one of the lines}\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The sets \\(V\\) and \\(H\\) are disjoint from the set of columns (resp. rows) covered by sunny lines.  \n   Because each point \\((c,n+1-c)\\in D_n\\) must be covered, we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus V = \\{c\\mid x=c\\text{ is missing}\\}=C,\n   \\qquad\n   \\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\setminus H = \\{c\\mid y=c\\text{ is missing}\\}=R,\n   \\]\n   and \\(|C|=|R|=k\\) (the number of sunny lines).\n\n5. **The uncovered sub‑grid.**  \n   Points whose column lies in \\(C\\) **and** whose row lies in \\(R\\) are not hit by any vertical or horizontal line.  \n   Inside this \\(k\\times k\\) grid the points belonging to \\(S_n\\) are exactly those strictly **below** the anti‑diagonal, i.e.\n\n   \\[\n   G:=\\{(c,r)\\mid c\\in C,\\;r\\in R,\\;c+r\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   After relabelling the elements of \\(C\\) as \\(c_i=n-i+1\\;(i=1,\\dots ,k)\\) and those of \\(R\\) as \\(r_i=i\\;(i=1,\\dots ,k)\\) (the only labelling compatible with the condition that \\((c_i,r_i)\\in D_n\\)), we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   G=\\bigl\\{(c_i,r_j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\le k\\bigr\\},\n   \\qquad |G|=\\frac{k(k-1)}2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Every point of \\(G\\) must be covered by a sunny line.\n\n6. **How many points can a sunny line cover inside \\(G\\)?**  \n   A sunny line is not vertical, hence it contains **at most one** point from each column.  \n   Consequently a sunny line can contain at most \\(k-1\\) points of \\(G\\) (it may miss the column that supplies its own anti‑diagonal point).  \n   With exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines the total number of points of \\(G\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\,(k-1).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Deriving a contradiction for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).**  \n   From (1) we need to cover \\(\\frac{k(k-1)}2\\) points, while (2) allows at most \\(k(k-1)\\) points – this does **no