t be the “reverse” of each other. This step therefore contains an unjustified assumption.\n\nThe rest of the argument that follows (the counting of \\(|G|\\) etc.) depends on this labeling, so the gap propagates.\n\n---\n\n### Step 6 – How many points can a sunny line cover inside \\(G\\)?\n> “A sunny line is not vertical, hence it contains **at most one** point from each column.  \n> Consequently a sunny line can contain at most \\(k-1\\) points of \\(G\\) (it may miss the column that supplies its own anti‑diagonal point).  \n> With exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines the total number of points of \\(G\\) that can be covered is at most \\(k(k-1)\\).”\n\n**Issue – Justification Gap:**  \n- While it is true that a non‑vertical line meets each vertical line in at most one point, the deduction that a sunny line can meet *all* but one of the \\(k\\) columns of \\(C\\) is not justified. A sunny line could, in principle, miss several columns of \\(C\\) depending on its slope and intercept. The bound \\(k(k-1)\\) is therefore not rigorously established.\n\n---\n\n### Step 7 – Deriving a contradiction for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n> “… a sunny line already uses one of the columns of \\(C\\) (the column of its anti‑diagonal point). In that column it cannot cover any point of \\(G\\) (all points of \\(G\\) lie **strictly below** the anti‑diagonal). Hence a sunny line can cover at most \\(k-2\\) points of \\(G\\).  \n> … the total capacity of the \\(k\\) sunny lines is therefore at most \\(k(k-2)\\).  \n> … For every integer \\(k\\ge 2\\), \\(k(k-2)<\\frac{k(k-1)}2\\) … which simplifies to \\(k<3\\).  \n> … we must sharpen the estimate. … a straight line can contain at most \\(\\lfloor k/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(G\\). … a more elementary way … any line meets \\(G\\) in at most two points, and because one of those two points would have to be the anti‑diagonal point (which is not in \\(G\\)), a sunny line meets \\(G\\) in **at most one** point.  \n> Consequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines can cover at most \\(k\\) points of \\(G\\). … we obtain \\(k\\le 2\\).”\n\n**Issues – Critical Errors (multiple):**  \n\n1. **Incorrect bound on points per line:**  \n   - The claim that a sunny line can meet at most one point of \\(G\\) is false. Counterexample: with \\(n=10\\), \\(k=5\\), the points \\((8,1)\\) and \\((6,2)\\) both belong to \\(G\\) and lie on the line of slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\), which is sunny. Hence a sunny line can contain **at least two** points of \\(G\\).  \n\n2. **Unjustified “only labelling” from Step 5:**  \n   - The argument that each column of \\(C\\) contains exactly \\(i-1\\) points of \\(G\\) (the lower‑triangular structure) depends on the specific ordering \\(c_i=n-i+1\\), \\(r_i=i\\). Since this ordering was not proved, the derived structure of \\(G\\) is not guaranteed.  \n\n3. **Faulty combinatorial inequality:**  \n   - Because the bound “at most one point per sunny line” is incorrect, the inequality \\(k\\le 2\\) does not follow. The subsequent conclusion that \\(k\\le 1\\) (or \\(k=n\\)) is therefore unsupported.\n\nThus the entire impossibility argument for \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\) collapses.\n\n---\n\n### Step 8 – The case \\(k=n\\)\n> “When every line is sunny, each line must meet the anti‑diagonal in a distinct point, so we may simply take, for each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\),  \n> \\(\\ell_i:\\) the line through \\((i,n+1-i)\\) and \\((i+1,n-i)\\) (with the convention that \\((n+1,0)\\) is omitted).  \n> Each \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(-\\frac{1}{i}\\) for \\(i<n\\) and slope \\(-\\frac{1}{n}\\) for \\(i=n\\); none of these slopes is \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\), so all lines are sunny.”\n\n**Issue – Critical Error:**  \n- The line through \\((i,n+1-i)\\) and \\((i+1,n-i)\\) has slope  \n  \\[\n  \\frac{(n-i)-(n+1-i)}{(i+1)-i}= \\frac{-1}{1} = -1,\n  \\]  \n  for **every** \\(i\\). Hence each \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(-1\\), which is explicitly prohibited for sunny lines. The construction therefore does **not** produce sunny lines, invalidating the claimed existence for \\(k=n\\).\n\n---\n\n### Step 9 – Constructions for the admissible values\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  > “Use the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\). All are non‑sunny and every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).”\n\n  **Verification:** This construction is correct; vertical lines are non‑sunny and indeed cover all required points.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  > “Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the sunny line  \n  \\(\\ell:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{n-1}\\,x + \\frac{n}{n-1},\\) which passes through the remaining anti‑diagonal point \\((n,1)\\) and is not parallel to any prohibited direction.”\n\n  **Issue – Critical Error:** Substituting \\(x=n\\) yields \\(y=0\\), not \\(y=1\\); thus the line does **not** pass through \\((n,1)\\). Consequently the point \\((n,1)\\) remains uncovered, and the construction fails.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).** (already addressed in Step 8 – critical error).\n\n---\n\n### Overall Assessment\nThe solution’s core counting argument (Steps 2–4) is based on a false claim about how many times a line can intersect the anti‑diagonal. Subsequent structural deductions about the sets of missing rows/columns, the size of the uncovered grid \\(G\\), and the maximal number of points a sunny line can cover are either unjustified or outright false. The impossibility proof for intermediate values of \\(k\\) collapses, and the explicit constructions for \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=n\\) are incorrect. Only the construction for \\(k=0\\) is valid.\n\nBecause the proof contains multiple **Critical Errors** that break the logical chain, the solution does **not** correctly solve the problem."
